bandit4@bandit:~/inhere$ cat ./-file07
4oQYVPkxZOOEOO5pTW81FB8j8lxXGUQw

Inside the `inhere` directory there are 10 files named `-file00` to `-file09`. The `file` command is used to determine the type of data a file contains (e.g. `ASCII text`, `data`, `ELF binary`) without needing to open it — useful when you don't know what a file holds. Since the filenames start with `-`, we use the `./` prefix to prevent the shell from misinterpreting them as flags. Rather than `cat`-ing each file one by one, we combine `file` with a wildcard `*` — running `file ./*` checks the data type of all files at once. Nine of them return `data` (binary, not human-readable) but `-file07` returns `ASCII text`, which is human-readable. We then read it with `cat ./-file07` to get the password.
The most common data encodings that are human-readable are ASCII and Unicode.

